#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "61bb0d60-a61e-5485-9df5-308e16dfd1aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5476" :question/problem "Determine if $1; 1$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "f4ff15d3-1a6e-56c0-a7c4-13f18ef1a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5477" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2$.\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0$.\n3. If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5$, then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n$Av|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}; = 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}; = 2v$.\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v}$, we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2$.\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n- Statement III is true. We have\n$A(2v) = 2Av = 2(5v) = 5(2v)$.\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5(2 \\mathbf{v})$, and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$, this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6cba45d6-5a69-5b83-937c-c19368aaeea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5478" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is $1; 2c$ Determine the value of $c$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= λv \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} &= 6c\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 + 2c \\\\ 1 + 4c\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6c \\\\ 12c^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2 + 2c = 6c; 1 + 4c = 12c^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 4c &= 12c^{2} \\\\\n1 + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} &= 12(\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n3 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "df40f37e-0d8f-5eb2-ad8a-9257440851df" :question/math-academy-id "e-5891" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2$. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/204140201746adea135bd375366ced72.png)" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$. So,\n$Av = 2v$.\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/ecd22d908f7242bad08169a51db3796d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02808189-8f96-53c7-879c-1bedd397bd7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103860} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd2dcb4-2ae3-5ae6-ba53-17fa6c9410c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103861} {:answer/id #uuid "245c7078-4e40-5ed3-90d8-00dc37077685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103862} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9ebb0c-3693-5995-a84f-0eb23c20eedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103863} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcf3f92-46cc-50fe-bfe9-749324312ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "245c7078-4e40-5ed3-90d8-00dc37077685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e26d64-6546-5ff0-a419-2696fe342729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103859}] :question/id #uuid "b4dcf6a4-b235-571e-8987-add1af756339" :question/math-academy-id "q-36791" :question/problem "Determine if $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9db08a0b-3982-5901-90c5-936ec024a06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103872} {:answer/id #uuid "bb02100d-0faa-5080-b26f-3a1e1f37effc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103873} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a883fa-f40b-50f2-aed9-590889bc3657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103874} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdfe14e-4427-52cc-b5aa-127f564fdd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103875} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffe6ce5-21ee-5b02-b86e-4dc6df16a4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9db08a0b-3982-5901-90c5-936ec024a06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103872} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7227bce-36c9-53c5-9bd0-8756564bb7ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103871}] :question/id #uuid "c27f53bd-6979-5472-9b95-f9c6b5cc1260" :question/math-academy-id "q-37087" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$.\n2. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-7$ then $3v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $λ =-7$.\n3. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0$."}]}
